🚀 Java vs JavaScript Cheatsheet (Functional Style)
1. Functions / Lambdas

Java

// Lambda as a variable
Supplier<String> msg = () -> "Hello Abhishek";
System.out.println(msg.get()); // Hello Abhishek


JavaScript

// Arrow function as a variable
const msg = () => "Hello Abhishek";
console.log(msg()); // Hello Abhishek

2. Iteration (classic vs functional)

Java

List<String> names = Arrays.asList("Abhishek", "Neha", "Arjun");

for (String name : names) {
    System.out.println(name);
}


JavaScript

const names = ["Abhishek", "Neha", "Arjun"];

for (let name of names) {
    console.log(name);
}


Or more functional:

names.forEach(name => console.log(name));

3. map (transforming a list)

Java

List<String> upper = names.stream()
    .map(String::toUpperCase)
    .collect(Collectors.toList());

System.out.println(upper); // [ABHISHEK, NEHA, ARJUN]


JavaScript

const upper = names.map(name => name.toUpperCase());
console.log(upper); // [ 'ABHISHEK', 'NEHA', 'ARJUN' ]

4. filter (selecting items)

Java

List<String> longNames = names.stream()
    .filter(n -> n.length() > 4)
    .collect(Collectors.toList());

System.out.println(longNames); // [Abhishek]


JavaScript

const longNames = names.filter(n => n.length > 4);
console.log(longNames); // [ 'Abhishek' ]

5. reduce (aggregation)

Java

int sum = Arrays.asList(1, 2, 3, 4, 5)
    .stream()
    .reduce(0, (a, b) -> a + b);

System.out.println(sum); // 15


JavaScript

const sum = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
    .reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);

console.log(sum); // 15

6. Optional vs Undefined/Null

Java

Optional<String> maybe = Optional.ofNullable(null);
System.out.println(maybe.orElse("Default")); // Default


JavaScript

let maybe = null;
console.log(maybe ?? "Default"); // Default


(?? = nullish coalescing operator, similar to Optional.orElse)

7. Method References vs Shorthand

Java

names.stream()
     .forEach(System.out::println);


JavaScript

names.forEach(console.log);
